  "descriptions": [
    {
      "source": "DeFiHackLabs",
      "text": "Read-Only-Reentrancy. Lost: ~$800k"
    },
    {
      "source": "REKT",
      "text": "Not so Sturdy, after all. Sturdy Finance has lost $800k to a price manipulation exploit made possible by a well-known read-only reentrancy vulnerability. Why is this issue still causing problems?"
    },
    {
      "source": "DeFiHackLabs explorer",
      "text": "setUserUseReserveAsCollateral() enables users to toggle whether or not an asset is used as collateral. Vulnerable code snippet: Attack tx: https://etherscan.io/tx/0xeb87ebc0a18aca7d2a9ffcabf61aa69c9e8d3c6efade9e2303f8857717fb9eb7 Analysis: https://sturdyfinance.medium.com/exploit-post-mortem-49261493307a"
    },
    {
      "source": "SlowMist",
      "text": "The DeFi lending protocol Sturdy is suspected to have been hacked, and information on the chain suggests that the attack may have been carried out through price manipulation. The attackers have transferred 442.6 ETH to Tornado Cash."
    },
    {
      "source": "RevertIQ",
      "text": "Sturdy Finance v1 on Ethereum Mainnet was exploited on June 12, 2023, via a read-only reentrancy flaw in its Balancer LP collateral logic. The attacker mispriced collateral by manipulating Balancer pool rates, extracting approximately $775,000 before the protocol was paused. The funds were ultimately returned after negotiation. The collateral toggle function setUserUseReserveAsCollateral did not enforce a reentrancy-locked oracle validation. This omission allowed the attacker to manipulate Balancer pool rates and borrow against inflated collateral value within a callback window."
    }
  ],
